    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The word republic comes from the Latin res publica, “public thing”, the government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> adopted after expelling its kings in the 6th century BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> praised it as a mixture of monarchy, aristocracy and democracy.</li><li>Republicanism holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> resides in the people and stresses civic virtue, the common good and the fight against corruption; modern scholars see it as a stream distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, which rests on economics and individualism.</li><li>Revived in the trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of late medieval and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it shaped the English Commonwealth, the American and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolutions</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> independence; today a republic usually means a democracy with an elected head of state.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a>’s Corsican Republic (1755–1769) is thought to have had the first constitution in the world to give women the vote, to female heads of family; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> wrote that “some day that little island will astonish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>”, and five American counties are named Paoli.</p></aside>
    </section>

